# Synopsis

Manage session hooks: shell scripts that run when the chat session enters or
leaves a state.

# Operations

- "create": create a hook. Requires "state" and "source". "mode" selects
  how the hook content is provided: "command" (source is the script text),
  "copy" (source is a file whose content is copied), "symlink" (source is
  a file the hook links to). "name" is optional (auto-generated when
  omitted); with "append" (requires "name"), the content is appended to
  the existing hook instead of failing.
- "remove": remove a hook. Requires "state" and "name"; "type" and "when"
  identify which hook is meant (defaulting to "oneshot"/"after", so pass
  them for hooks created with other values).
- "list-enabled": list enabled hooks. "state", "type" and "when" can be
  used to filter the list.
- "path": print the absolute pathname of a hook. Requires "state" and
  "name"; "type" and "when" identify which hook is meant (defaulting to
  "oneshot"/"after", so pass them for hooks created with other values).

# States

any-state, read-loop, agent-loop-api-call, agent-loop-tool-call,
agent-loop-end, agent-loop-api-call-interrupted,
agent-loop-tool-call-interrupted, end.

"any-state" is a wildcard: its hooks run on every state transition, in
addition to the hooks of the state being entered or left.

# Approval

"create" and "remove" require approval on the controlling terminal. In
non-interactive sessions they are rejected unless automatic approval is
configured.

# Returns

A JSON object. On success: create returns
{"created":"<rel>","path":...} ({"appended":"<rel>","path":...} when
appending); remove returns {"removed":"<rel>","path":...}; list-enabled
returns {"hooks":[{"rel":...,"path":...},...]}; path returns {"path":...}.
On failure: {"error": <reason>}; when rejected: {"rejected": <reason>}.

<rel> is the relative hook path in the form state/type/when/name.

# Notes

Use this tool to install scripts that run automatically on session state
changes (for example desktop notifications or log appends), not to run
commands directly; use the shell tool for that. Hook output goes to the
user's terminal, not to the model.
